#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139507} :activity/id #uuid "b0017abd-99d5-54d3-8a58-5379b4b38c75" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049200 :topic/id #uuid "e2ea8cb9-99ed-50a0-9f84-e14a154720d8" :topic/math-academy-id 2270} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139664 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation with a decimal coefficient, such as \n\n$$\n0.2x = -1,\n$$\n\nwe start by getting rid of the decimal. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${\\color{blue}10}.$ Multiplying a decimal by $10$ shifts the decimal place one spot over to the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2x & =-1 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.2x) & =10\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-10 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{-10}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & =-\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b3690d9a-5c80-54c4-b523-5aef327aa8f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8147 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1fb7ee48-ceed-57fd-b2ac-c96be2c76bf9" :step/math-academy-id 77041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138526 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138077 :question/id #uuid "6ff19222-3f56-53cf-bd39-b399573664b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13026"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0576504-7bd6-5649-99fe-a758faaffa4b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487347}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Containing Single-Digit Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "364c2756-fa89-54cc-b2db-95feab14b48c" :step/math-academy-id 77042 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139665 :tutorial/content "How do we get rid of the decimal in an equation like\n\n$$\n0.25x = 2,\n$$\n\nwhen there is more than one number behind the decimal place? If we multiply by $10$ as usual, we shift the decimal place over one spot to the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25x & =2 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.25x) & =10\\cdot (2) \\\\ 2.5x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is still one number behind the decimal place. So, we can multiply by $10$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5x & =20 \\\\ 10\\cdot (2.5x) & =10\\cdot (20) \\\\ 25x & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that our equation contains only whole numbers, we can continue with the usual method of applying the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25x & =200 \\\\ \\frac{25x}{25} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{25}x}{\\cancel{25}} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking back, we can see a faster way to apply the same method. We multiplied by $10$ twice, which means that overall, we multiplied by $100.$ In general, multiplying a decimal by $100$ shifts the decimal place over two spots to the right. So, we could have solved the equation faster by multiplying by $100.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25x & =2 \\\\ 100\\cdot (0.25x) & =100\\cdot (2) \\\\ 25x & =200 \\\\ \\frac{25x}{25} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{25}x}{\\cancel{25}} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "50c650c0-be15-5aa0-87c5-181d41d15934" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8148 :tutorial/title "Solving Linear Equations with More than One Decimal Place"} :step/id #uuid "82c64b9e-8aa0-5f1b-8c24-1c35d17dc86b" :step/math-academy-id 77046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138078 :question/id #uuid "015eac36-e18d-5a53-bfff-f301722a1f0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13027"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "039fc0a8-337f-50b3-be2c-cfbdbd399537" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487356}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Containing Two-Digit Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "f7c6198b-012e-5cff-b87c-52603f1f404e" :step/math-academy-id 77047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138079 :question/id #uuid "49ed1c21-223b-5dda-8f4d-7b6a3492a26b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13028"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68f57411-1d39-52cd-86ee-abf633adf3c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487372}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b47f949a-8886-5418-81d1-5442ddd763e1" :step/math-academy-id 77051}] :activity/title "Solving Linear Equations With Decimal Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138597}]}
